{"title":"Glacier Meals","sub":"The packing robot that wouldn't add a single meal","type":"Cost & operations","industry":"Food manufacturing","geo":"Canada","diff":2,"fmt":"Candidate-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"Bottlenecks · wrong-fix trap","prompt":"Glacier makes frozen ready meals in Ontario for Canadian grocers. Demand is 440,000 trays a year and growing, and Glacier is turning down orders. The operations director proposes a $600k automated packing line, because packing is the most labour-intensive step. The CEO asks you to evaluate it.","clarify":[{"q":"Process?","a":"Prep ingredients, cook, blast-freeze, pack. Trays move in sequence."},{"q":"Hours?","a":"Two shifts, 3,200 hours a year."},{"q":"Quality?","a":"96% of trays pass inspection."},{"q":"Economics?","a":"Each extra tray sold earns about $4 of contribution."}],"structure":{"root":"How should Glacier increase output?","branches":[{"head":"Capacity","items":["Trays per hour at each step","Bottleneck","What shifts after each upgrade"]},{"head":"Options","items":["Packing robot","Faster cooking","More blast-freezer capacity"]},{"head":"Economics","items":["Extra trays × $4","Payback"]}]},"hypothesis":"Labour-intensive doesn't mean bottleneck. If packing is already faster than cooking, a robot will save labour but won't add any meals.","questions":[{"q":"Here are the step speeds. What would the packing robot (+25% packing speed) do to output?","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"399.26pt\" height=\"164.94pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 399.26 164.94\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\"1.1\"><defs><style type=\"text/css\">*{stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: butt}</style></defs><g id=\"figure_1\"><g id=\"patch_1\"><path d=\"M 0 164.94 L 399.26 164.94 L 399.26 0 L 0 0 L 0 164.94 z\n\" style=\"fill: none\"/></g><g id=\"axes_1\"><g id=\"patch_2\"><path d=\"M 46.1 139.04 L 392.06 139.04 L 392.06 11.528 L 46.1 11.528 L 46.1 139.04 z\n\" style=\"fill: